import pf.Html
import ../../../Ui/Code
import ../../../Ui/Heading
import ../../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../../Generated/Sources
import ../../../Generated/Paths

## The vibrate package: the phone's vibration motor from a handler (`Vibrate.once!`,
## `Vibrate.pattern!`), the plugin whose script is the browser's half (`Vibrator.plugin`), and the
## Android host's own half. The pomodoro example buzzes with it when a session ends.
meta = {
	title: "Vibrate",
	description: "packages/vibrate, a plugin for Roc Solid apps: the phone's vibration motor buzzed from a handler, once or in a pattern, in a browser (navigator.vibrate) and in an Android app on the native host.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "install", label: "Installing it" },
		{ id: "buzz", label: "Buzzing" },
		{ id: "browser", label: "In a browser" },
		{ id: "phone", label: "On a phone" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="The ecosystem · vibrate"
				title="A buzz from a handler"
			>
				<code>{"Vibrate.pattern!([300, 150, 300])"}</code> buzzes the phone twice: 300 ms on, 150 ms off,
				300 ms on. The same line works in Chrome on Android and in an app on the
				<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>Android host</a>.
			</PageHero>
			<p>
				Use it when a phone should buzz: a timer ran out, a message arrived, a tap should be felt. Call
				<code>Vibrate.once!</code> or <code>Vibrate.pattern!</code> from a handler. The
				<a href={Paths.examples_pomodoro}>pomodoro example</a> buzzes when a session ends.
			</p>
			<p>
				<code>Vibrate</code> is a module both halves of an app compile, so a component that buzzes
				type-checks on the server and in the browser. <code>Vibrator</code> is a
				<a href={"${Paths.platform_server}#plugins"}>plugin</a> that serves a small script every page loads,
				which passes the buzz to the browser. An Android app on the native host needs no script: the host
				runs the buzz itself.
			</p>

			<Heading id="install">Installing it</Heading>
			<p>Name the package in the app's header, and add the plugin to the app's plugins:</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_header.file}, its header"} source={Sources.vibrate_header.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_install.file}, in part"} source={Sources.vibrate_install.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />
			<p>Then import <code>vibrate.Vibrate</code> wherever you buzz.</p>

			<Heading id="buzz">Buzzing</Heading>
			<p>The whole module is two functions:</p>
			<Code title={Sources.vibrate_module.file} source={Sources.vibrate_module.rocx} />
			<ul>
				<li>
					<code>{"Vibrate.once!(400)"}</code> buzzes once, for 400 milliseconds.
				</li>
				<li>
					<code>{"Vibrate.pattern!([200, 100, 200])"}</code> takes durations in milliseconds, alternating on
					and off, as <code>navigator.vibrate</code> takes them: buzz, rest, buzz.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A step longer than 10 seconds is cut to 10, and only the first 32 steps play.
			</p>
			<p>
				A buzz is a <a href={"${Paths.components}#handlers"}><code>Browser.call!</code></a> underneath, with the
				same rules. It runs after the handler finishes and the page shows what it changed. Only a handler can
				buzz: an event's handler, an <a href={"${Paths.components}#effects"}>effect's</a> <code>apply</code>,
				or a timer's callback. Calling it while the page computes (a hole, a memo or a component's body), or
				on the server, fails. The pomodoro's timer buzzes from its interval's tick, beside its chime:
			</p>
			<Code title={"${Sources.vibrate_use.file}, in part"} source={Sources.vibrate_use.rocx} numbers={Bool.False} />

			<Heading id="browser">In a browser</Heading>
			<p>
				The plugin serves its script at <code>/rocx/plugins/vibrate/vibrate.js</code>. Every page loads it
				before <code>start.js</code>, so it is ready before the page hydrates. It registers the browser effect
				<code>vibrate.pattern</code> on <code>window.rocxEffects</code>, which passes the pattern to
				<code>navigator.vibrate</code>:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.vibrate_script.file} source={Sources.vibrate_script.rocx} />
			<p>
				Chrome on Android buzzes. Desktop browsers, Safari and Firefox give pages no motor, so there a buzz
				does nothing, and nothing fails. A browser may also stay still until the reader has touched the page;
				that isn't an error either.
			</p>

			<Heading id="phone">On a phone</Heading>
			<p>
				On the native Android host, a plugin's <code>Browser.call!</code> goes to a native handler of the same
				name, with no script. The host has two: <code>audio.play</code> (for
				<a href={Paths.ecosystem_audio}>audio</a>, a MediaPlayer that fetches the sound from the app's server)
				and <code>vibrate.pattern</code>, which plays the pattern on Android's <code>Vibrator</code> as a
				waveform. The app's manifest asks for <code>android.permission.VIBRATE</code>, which Android grants at
				install, so the user sees no prompt.
			</p>
		</NoHydration>
	</>
